    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cañones traces its roots to 1766 when Juan Pablo Martín Serrano received the Polvadera Grant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ca%C3%B1ones%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Serrano, a wealthy military veteran, set up a seasonal ranch in the canyon, raising livestock, farming the bottom, and trading with the Utes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ca%C3%B1ones%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cañones kept its own post office until January 3, 2002, when it finally closed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ca%C3%B1ones%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the 1960s, anthropologists Paul Kutsche and John R. Van Ness visited Cañones, noting it as a classic example of the Rio Arriba Hispanic subculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ca%C3%B1ones%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Just south of Cañones, the ruins of Tsiping—or Tsi’pinouinge—lie on Pueblo Mesa, offering a glimpse into pre‑historic life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
